.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH stamp-suite 1  "stamp-suite 1.0.0" 
.SH NAME
stamp\-suite \- Simple Two\-Way Active Measurement Protocol (STAMP)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBstamp\-suite\fR [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-print\-config\-schema\fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-\-remote\-addr\fR] [\fB\-S\fR|\fB\-\-local\-addr\fR] [\fB\-\-local\-scope\-id\fR] [\fB\-\-remote\-scope\-id\fR] [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-remote\-port\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-local\-port\fR] [\fB\-K\fR|\fB\-\-clock\-source\fR] [\fB\-\-clock\-sync\-source\fR] [\fB\-\-hardware\-clock\-sync\-source\fR] [\fB\-\-reflector\-utc\-offset\fR] [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-send\-delay\fR] [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-count\fR] [\fB\-L\fR|\fB\-\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-session\-loss\-threshold\fR] [\fB\-A\fR|\fB\-\-auth\-mode\fR] [\fB\-R \fR] [\fB\-i\fR|\fB\-\-is\-reflector\fR] [\fB\-\-error\-scale\fR] [\fB\-\-error\-multiplier\fR] [\fB\-\-clock\-synchronized\fR] [\fB\-\-hmac\-key\fR] [\fB\-\-hmac\-key\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-hmac\-key\-dir\fR] [\fB\-\-require\-hmac\fR] [\fB\-\-strict\-packets\fR] [\fB\-\-stateful\-reflector\fR] [\fB\-\-session\-admission\fR] [\fB\-\-reflector\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-session\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-allowed\-dscp\fR] [\fB\-\-allowed\-ecn\fR] [\fB\-\-allowed\-dscp\-for\fR] [\fB\-\-drop\-replayed\fR] [\fB\-\-location\-disclose\fR] [\fB\-\-tlv\-mode\fR] [\fB\-\-verify\-tlv\-hmac\fR] [\fB\-\-ssid\fR] [\fB\-\-on\-zero\-ssid\fR] [\fB\-\-metrics\fR] [\fB\-\-metrics\-addr\fR] [\fB\-\-cos\fR] [\fB\-\-dscp\fR] [\fB\-\-ecn\fR] [\fB\-\-ecn\-backoff\-factor\fR] [\fB\-\-ecn\-max\-delay\fR] [\fB\-\-ecn\-recovery\-step\fR] [\fB\-\-ttl\fR] [\fB\-\-malformed\fR] [\fB\-\-access\-report\fR] [\fB\-\-access\-return\-code\fR] [\fB\-\-access\-report\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-access\-report\-retries\fR] [\fB\-\-timestamp\-info\fR] [\fB\-\-direct\-measurement\fR] [\fB\-\-location\fR] [\fB\-\-follow\-up\-telemetry\fR] [\fB\-\-snmp\fR] [\fB\-\-snmp\-socket\fR] [\fB\-\-control\fR] [\fB\-\-control\-addr\fR] [\fB\-\-control\-token\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-control\-tls\-cert\fR] [\fB\-\-control\-tls\-key\fR] [\fB\-\-output\-format\fR] [\fB\-\-log\-format\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-\-hwtstamp\fR] [\fB\-\-report\-interval\fR] [\fB\-\-dest\-node\-addr\fR] [\fB\-\-return\-path\-cc\fR] [\fB\-\-return\-address\fR] [\fB\-\-return\-sr\-mpls\-labels\fR] [\fB\-\-return\-srv6\-sids\fR] [\fB\-\-srv6\-return\-forwarding\fR] [\fB\-\-return\-path\-allow\-alternate\fR] [\fB\-\-micro\-session\-id\fR] [\fB\-\-reflector\-member\-link\-id\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-pps\fR] [\fB\-\-reflector\-rate\-burst\fR] [\fB\-\-reflector\-queue\-capacity\fR] [\fB\-\-reflector\-shutdown\-grace\-ms\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-sessions\fR] [\fB\-\-ber\fR] [\fB\-\-extra\-padding\fR] [\fB\-\-ber\-omit\-burst\fR] [\fB\-\-tlv\-hmac\fR] [\fB\-\-ber\-pattern\fR] [\fB\-\-ber\-padding\-size\fR] [\fB\-\-ber\-interval\fR] [\fB\-\-ber\-bit\-threshold\fR] [\fB\-\-ber\-packet\-threshold\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-count\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-length\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-interval\-ns\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-no\-ext\-hdr\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-max\-count\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-max\-size\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-min\-interval\-ns\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-fixed\-hdr\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-ipv6\-ext\-hdr\fR] [\fB\-\-attach\-ext\-hdr\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-ipv6\-ext\-hdr\-selector\fR] [\fB\-\-reflected\-fixed\-hdr\-selector\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Simple Two\-Way Active Measurement Protocol (STAMP)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Path to a TOML configuration file. Values loaded from the file are used as defaults; command\-line flags and environment variables always override them
.TP
\fB\-\-print\-config\-schema\fR
Print the TOML configuration\*(Aqs JSON Schema to stdout and exit.

`stamp\-suite \-\-print\-config\-schema > stamp\-suite\-config.schema.json`

Use the schema with an IDE or validator; JSON validators require converting TOML input to JSON first.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-remote\-addr\fR \fI<REMOTE_ADDR>\fR [default: 0.0.0.0]
Remote address for Session Reflector
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-local\-addr\fR \fI<LOCAL_ADDR>\fR [default: 0.0.0.0]
Local address to bind for
.TP
\fB\-\-local\-scope\-id\fR \fI<LOCAL_SCOPE_ID>\fR [default: 0]
Numeric IPv6 interface zone for the local bind address (0 = default)
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\-scope\-id\fR \fI<REMOTE_SCOPE_ID>\fR [default: 0]
Numeric IPv6 interface zone for the sender destination (0 = default)
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-remote\-port\fR \fI<REMOTE_PORT>\fR [default: 862]
UDP port number for outgoing packets
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-local\-port\fR \fI<LOCAL_PORT>\fR [default: 0]
Local UDP port: randomized dynamic sender port (0), or 862 for a reflector
.TP
\fB\-K\fR, \fB\-\-clock\-source\fR \fI<CLOCK_SOURCE>\fR [default: NTP]
Timestamp wire encoding (NTP or PTP); does not configure clock synchronization
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
NTP: Network Time Protocol timestamp format (RFC 5905)
.IP \(bu 2
PTP: Precision Time Protocol timestamp format (IEEE 1588)
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-clock\-sync\-source\fR \fI<CLOCK_SYNC_SOURCE>\fR [default: local]
Reflector: declared synchronization source of the system clock (Type 3 TLV). Independent of \-\-clock\-source and \-\-clock\-synchronized; no clock service is probed
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
ntp
.IP \(bu 2
ptp
.IP \(bu 2
gps
.IP \(bu 2
glonass
.IP \(bu 2
loran\-c
.IP \(bu 2
bds
.IP \(bu 2
galileo
.IP \(bu 2
local: No external synchronization source is asserted (the default)
.IP \(bu 2
ssu\-bits
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-hardware\-clock\-sync\-source\fR \fI<HARDWARE_CLOCK_SYNC_SOURCE>\fR [default: local]
Reflector: declared synchronization source of NIC hardware clocks used for T2. Only used when a received timestamp actually comes from hardware. Ensure the PHC is aligned with the system clock; this setting does not synchronize it
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
ntp
.IP \(bu 2
ptp
.IP \(bu 2
gps
.IP \(bu 2
glonass
.IP \(bu 2
loran\-c
.IP \(bu 2
bds
.IP \(bu 2
galileo
.IP \(bu 2
local: No external synchronization source is asserted (the default)
.IP \(bu 2
ssu\-bits
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-reflector\-utc\-offset\fR \fI<REFLECTOR_UTC_OFFSET>\fR [default: 0]
Sender: seconds the reflector clock is ahead of UTC after epoch conversion. Subtracted from T2/T3 for one\-way delay; zero for this suite\*(Aqs UTC clocks. Set from the peer\*(Aqs time configuration (e.g. its TAI\-UTC offset), not its Z bit
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-send\-delay\fR \fI<SEND_DELAY>\fR [default: 1000]
Delay between next packets
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-count\fR \fI<COUNT>\fR [default: 1000]
Count of packets to be sent
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<TIMEOUT>\fR [default: 5]
Amount of time to wait for packet until consider it lost (in seconds)
.TP
\fB\-\-session\-loss\-threshold\fR \fI<SESSION_LOSS_THRESHOLD>\fR [default: 3]
Consecutive unanswered probes before an active session is reported failed. Uses \-\-timeout per probe; 0 timeout disables loss\-driven state changes
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-auth\-mode\fR \fI<AUTH_MODE>\fR [default: O]
Specify work mode \- A for authenticated, O for open (unauthenticated) \- default "O"
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
A: Authenticated mode \- packets include HMAC for integrity verification
.IP \(bu 2
O: Open (unauthenticated) mode \- packets are sent without HMAC authentication
.RE
.TP
\fB\-R\fR
Print individual packet statistics (stderr for JSON/CSV output, stdout for text)
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-is\-reflector\fR
Run as Session Reflector instead of Session Sender
.TP
\fB\-\-error\-scale\fR \fI<ERROR_SCALE>\fR [default: 0]
Error estimate scale (0\-63). Default: 0
.TP
\fB\-\-error\-multiplier\fR \fI<ERROR_MULTIPLIER>\fR [default: 1]
Error estimate multiplier (0\-255). Default: 1
.TP
\fB\-\-clock\-synchronized\fR
Assert the Error Estimate S bit; independent of wire format and Type 3 source
.TP
\fB\-\-hmac\-key\fR \fI<HMAC_KEY>\fR
HMAC key in hex; requires at least 32 hex chars (16 bytes). 64 hex chars (32 bytes) matches the HMAC\-SHA\-256 output length.

CLI keys are visible in process arguments; environment keys are visible to users who can read the process environment. Prefer `\-\-hmac\-key\-file` in production. The stored value is zeroized on drop and redacted from `Debug`.
.TP
\fB\-\-hmac\-key\-file\fR \fI<HMAC_KEY_FILE>\fR
Path to file containing HMAC key
.TP
\fB\-\-hmac\-key\-dir\fR \fI<HMAC_KEY_DIR>\fR
Directory of per\-SSID HMAC keys. File stems are hexadecimal SSIDs; `default.key` supplies the fallback. Conflicts with `\-\-hmac\-key` and `\-\-hmac\-key\-file`. Restart with updated files to rotate keys
.TP
\fB\-\-require\-hmac\fR
Require HMAC key to be configured (error if missing in auth mode). Note: When an HMAC key is present, verification is always mandatory per RFC 8762 §4.4
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\-packets\fR
Reject short packets instead of zero\-filling (RFC 8762 Section 4.6). By default, missing bytes are zero\-filled for TWAMP\-Light interoperability
.TP
\fB\-\-stateful\-reflector\fR
Enable stateful reflector mode per RFC 8762 Section 4.2. The reflector maintains independent sequence counters for each full session identity instead of echoing the sender\*(Aqs sequence number, allowing clients to detect reflector\-side packet loss
.TP
\fB\-\-session\-admission\fR \fI<SESSION_ADMISSION>\fR [default: permissive]
Session admission: permissive learns incoming sessions; provisioned only answers exact \-\-reflector\-session identities (RFC 8972 Section 3)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
permissive: Learn sessions from incoming packets (legacy interoperability mode)
.IP \(bu 2
provisioned: Only answer explicitly provisioned session identities
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-reflector\-session\fR \fI<SSID,SOURCE,DESTINATION[,MICRO_ID]>\fR
Provision an exact session; repeat for each tuple/SSID/member. SSID 0 selects a base session without an explicit SSID. IPv6 uses brackets
.TP
\fB\-\-session\-timeout\fR \fI<SESSION_TIMEOUT>\fR [default: 300]
Session timeout in seconds for reflector runtime state. Sessions inactive for this duration may be cleaned up. Default: 300 (5 minutes). Set to 0 to disable
.TP
\fB\-\-allowed\-dscp\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR [default: all]
DSCP codepoints permitted on reflector replies (RFC 8972 §4.4/§6, cos\-ecn\-01 §3.2): `all` (default), `none`, or values and inclusive ranges such as `0,8,10\-14,46`. Rejected DSCP1 requests retain the received DSCP and report RPD=0b01. Permitted values still require socket support.

Reflector\-side only.
.TP
\fB\-\-allowed\-ecn\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR [default: all]
ECN codepoints the reflector may apply to a reply\*(Aqs IP header when a Class of Service TLV requests them (cos\-ecn\-01 §3.2).

`all` (default), `none`, or a comma\-separated list of 0\-3. A refused EC1 forces the reply\*(Aqs ECN bits to Not\-ECT and the echoed TLV reports RPE=0b10.

Reflector\-side only.
.TP
\fB\-\-allowed\-dscp\-for\fR \fI<PREFIX/LEN=SPEC>\fR
Destination DSCP policy overriding `\-\-allowed\-dscp` (cos\-ecn\-01 §3.2). Repeatable `PREFIX/LEN=SPEC`, e.g. `\-\-allowed\-dscp\-for 192.0.2.0/24=0,46`. The longest matching prefix replaces the global set, regardless of order.

Reflector\-side only.
.TP
\fB\-\-drop\-replayed\fR
Suppress duplicated packets without a handled Type\-12 request.

Non\-monotonic Type\-12 requests always receive a single U\-flagged reply after validation (draft\-ietf\-ippm\-asymmetrical\-pkts\-14 §5), even with this flag set. Detection and counting are always active. Off by default: restarting a sender can repeat sequence numbers without an attack.

Reflector\-side only.
.TP
\fB\-\-location\-disclose\fR \fI<FIELDS>\fR [default: all]
Location TLV fields the reflector may report (RFC 8972 §4.2.2). Comma\-separated: `all` (default), `none`, or `src\-port`, `dst\-port`, `ports`, `src\-ip`, `dst\-ip`, `ips`. Withheld fields are zeroed without changing the TLV length or structure.

Reflector\-side only; ignored by the sender.
.TP
\fB\-\-tlv\-mode\fR \fI<TLV_MODE>\fR [default: echo]
TLV handling mode for the reflector (RFC 8972). Default: echo. \- ignore: Strip TLVs from reflected packets (zero\-pad to preserve length) \- echo: Echo TLVs back, marking unknown types with U\-flag
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
ignore: Ignore TLVs \- strip them from reflected packets (zero\-pad to preserve length)
.IP \(bu 2
echo: Echo TLVs back to sender, marking unknown types with U\-flag per RFC 8972
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-verify\-tlv\-hmac\fR
Verify HMAC TLV in incoming packets (RFC 8972). Requires HMAC key
.TP
\fB\-\-ssid\fR \fI<SSID>\fR
Session\-Sender Identifier to include in sender packets (RFC 8972 §3). Encoded in the two octets of the base STAMP header immediately after Error Estimate (bytes 14\-15 unauth / 26\-27 auth)
.TP
\fB\-\-on\-zero\-ssid\fR \fI<ACTION>\fR [default: continue]
What to do when a reflected packet returns a zeroed SSID field (RFC 8972 §3): `continue` (default) keeps measuring and logs the condition once, `stop` ends the session on the first such reply.

Sender\-side only, and only meaningful together with a non\-zero `\-\-ssid` — without one there is nothing for the reflector to echo.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
continue: Keep measuring, logging the condition once. The RFC permits continuing, and it is the useful default for a probe pointed at an unknown peer
.IP \(bu 2
stop: Stop the session on the first zeroed\-SSID reply. For an operator who requires SSID\-demultiplexed sessions, a reflector that drops the field makes the measurement meaningless
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\fR
Enable Prometheus metrics endpoint (requires "metrics" feature)
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\-addr\fR \fI<METRICS_ADDR>\fR [default: 127.0.0.1:9090]
Address to bind the metrics HTTP server
.TP
\fB\-\-cos\fR
Enable Class of Service TLV for DSCP/ECN measurement (RFC 8972 §4.4). When enabled, the sender includes a CoS TLV with the requested DSCP/ECN values, and the reflector reports the received DSCP/ECN values
.TP
\fB\-\-dscp\fR \fI<DSCP>\fR [default: 0]
DSCP value to request for reflected packets (0\-63). Only used when \-\-cos is enabled. Common values: 0=Best Effort, 10=AF11, 18=AF21, 26=AF31, 34=AF41, 46=EF
.TP
\fB\-\-ecn\fR \fI<ECN>\fR [default: 0]
ECN value to request for reflected packets (0\-3). Only used when \-\-cos is enabled. 0=Not\-ECT, 1=ECT(1), 2=ECT(0), 3=CE (Congestion Experienced)
.TP
\fB\-\-ecn\-backoff\-factor\fR \fI<ECN_BACKOFF_FACTOR>\fR [default: 2]
Multiply the send interval by this factor on each CE\-marked reply, capped at `\-\-ecn\-max\-delay` (draft\-ietf\-ippm\-stamp\-cos\-ecn\-01 §3.4). Must exceed 1.0. Active with `\-\-cos` and `\-\-ecn` ECT0 (2) or ECT1 (1). See `\-\-ecn\-recovery\-step` for recovery pacing
.TP
\fB\-\-ecn\-max\-delay\fR \fI<ECN_MAX_DELAY>\fR [default: 30000]
Upper bound (milliseconds) on the AIMD\-controlled send interval — caps how far repeated CE observations can back the sender off (draft\-ietf\-ippm\-stamp\-cos\-ecn\-01 §3.4). Must be >= `\-\-send\-delay` when the controller is active
.TP
\fB\-\-ecn\-recovery\-step\fR \fI<ECN_RECOVERY_STEP>\fR [default: 50]
Additive recovery step (milliseconds): after each reply that was NOT CE\-marked, the AIMD\-controlled send interval shrinks by this amount, down to (never below) `\-\-send\-delay` (draft\-ietf\-ippm\-stamp\-cos\-ecn\-01 §3.4)
.TP
\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fI<TTL>\fR
Outgoing TTL / Hop Limit. Draft ext\-hdr\-13 requires 255 (the default)
.TP
\fB\-\-malformed\fR \fI<MALFORMED>\fR
Diagnostic: append a deliberately malformed TLV to every sent packet to test a reflector\*(Aqs RFC 8972 §4.2 handling. `bad\-flags` sets reserved flag bits; `bad\-length` declares a TLV length that overruns the packet. Not for normal measurements
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
bad\-flags: A structurally valid TLV whose flags octet has reserved bits set (RFC 8972 §4.2 requires reserved bits to be zero)
.IP \(bu 2
bad\-length: A TLV whose Length field far exceeds the bytes actually present, so a conformant reflector must set the M\-flag and stop processing
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-access\-report\fR \fI<ACCESS_REPORT>\fR
Enable Access Report TLV with Access ID 1\-15 (RFC 8972 §4.6). IDs 1 (3GPP Network) and 2 (Non\-3GPP Network) are defined; 3\-15 are accepted with a startup warning. Zero is rejected. The reflector echoes valid reports unchanged
.TP
\fB\-\-access\-return\-code\fR \fI<ACCESS_RETURN_CODE>\fR [default: 1]
Return code for Access Report TLV (default: 1 = available). Only used when \-\-access\-report is enabled
.TP
\fB\-\-access\-report\-timeout\fR \fI<ACCESS_REPORT_TIMEOUT>\fR [default: 3]
Access Report retransmission timeout in seconds (RFC 8972 §4.6; default 3). Armed after sending the TLV and disarmed on its reflected echo. Expiry triggers retransmission. Requires `\-\-access\-report`
.TP
\fB\-\-access\-report\-retries\fR \fI<ACCESS_REPORT_RETRIES>\fR [default: 4]
Maximum Access Report retransmissions (RFC 8972 §4.6; default 4). Zero aborts on the first missed acknowledgment without retransmitting. Requires `\-\-access\-report`
.TP
\fB\-\-timestamp\-info\fR
Enable Timestamp Information TLV (RFC 8972 §4.3). The sender requests the reflector\*(Aqs synchronization sources and timestamp methods using zeroed information fields; the reflector fills in its values
.TP
\fB\-\-direct\-measurement\fR
Enable Direct Measurement TLV (RFC 8972 §4.5). The sender includes its transmit count; the reflector fills receive and transmit counters
.TP
\fB\-\-location\fR
Enable Location TLV (RFC 8972 §4.2). The reflector fills in the observed source/destination addresses and ports
.TP
\fB\-\-follow\-up\-telemetry\fR
Enable Follow\-Up Telemetry TLV (RFC 8972 §4.7). The reflector fills in the previous reflection\*(Aqs sequence number and timestamp
.TP
\fB\-\-snmp\fR
Enable SNMP AgentX sub\-agent (requires "snmp" feature)
.TP
\fB\-\-snmp\-socket\fR \fI<SNMP_SOCKET>\fR [default: /var/agentx/master]
AgentX master agent socket path
.TP
\fB\-\-control\fR
Enable the runtime control\-plane REST API (reflector only; requires the "control" build feature). Design: doc/control\-plane.md
.TP
\fB\-\-control\-addr\fR \fI<ADDR>\fR [default: 127.0.0.1:9091]
Address to bind the control\-plane HTTP server. Keep this on loopback unless network\-level access control is in place
.TP
\fB\-\-control\-token\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Path to a file containing a static bearer token. When set, every control\-plane request must carry `Authorization: Bearer <token>`
.TP
\fB\-\-control\-tls\-cert\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
PEM certificate chain for the control API. Enables HTTPS and requires `\-\-control\-tls\-key` and a bearer token for session/key management and shutdown
.TP
\fB\-\-control\-tls\-key\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
PEM private key matching `\-\-control\-tls\-cert`
.TP
\fB\-\-output\-format\fR \fI<OUTPUT_FORMAT>\fR [default: text]
Statistics format on stdout (text, JSON lines, or CSV with one header per run)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
text: Human\-readable text output
.IP \(bu 2
json: JSON output for machine consumption
.IP \(bu 2
csv: CSV output for spreadsheet import
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-format\fR \fI<LOG_FORMAT>\fR [default: text]
Diagnostic log format on stderr — `text` (default) for journalctl\-friendly human\-readable lines, `json` for structured one\-line\-per\-event output suitable for log aggregators. `RUST_LOG` continues to control verbosity in both modes
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
text: Human\-readable single\-line output (the default; matches the historic `env_logger` style)
.IP \(bu 2
json: Structured JSON, one event per line. Suitable for ingestion by log shippers (Fluent Bit, Vector, journald JSON forwarder)
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase log verbosity: `\-v` for debug, `\-vv` or more for trace. Defaults to info. A non\-empty `RUST_LOG` overrides this flag
.TP
\fB\-\-hwtstamp\fR \fI<HWTSTAMP>\fR [default: auto]
Kernel/hardware timestamp handling (requires the "hwtstamp" build feature for the read paths). `auto` (default): kernel software timestamps — precise T2/T4 and error\-queue TX correction on Linux, SO_TIMESTAMP receive timestamps on macOS; no privileges, no NIC changes. `on`: additionally attempt NIC hardware timestamping (SIOCSHWTSTAMP + raw\-hardware tier; needs CAP_NET_ADMIN and a synchronized PHC), warning + software fallback when unavailable. `off`: userspace timestamps only
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: Use kernel *software* timestamps when the build supports them (feature "hwtstamp"); transparently fall back to userspace timestamps otherwise. Never reconfigures the NIC and needs no privileges. This is the default — safe to leave on every host
.IP \(bu 2
on: Additionally attempt NIC *hardware* timestamping: sets the NIC timestamp filters (SIOCSHWTSTAMP, needs CAP_NET_ADMIN) and requests the raw\-hardware tier, falling back to kernel software timestamps with a warning when any step fails. Operators are responsible for PHC synchronization (ptp4l/phc2sys) — see doc/architecture.md
.IP \(bu 2
off: Always use software timestamping, even when HW is available. Useful for A/B\-style measurement comparisons or as a fallback when a particular NIC\*(Aqs HW path is suspect
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-report\-interval\fR \fI<REPORT_INTERVAL>\fR [default: 0]
Periodic reporting interval in seconds (0 = disabled, sender only)
.TP
\fB\-\-dest\-node\-addr\fR \fI<IP>\fR
Destination Node Address for SR networks (RFC 9503 §3). Requires \-\-ssid
.TP
\fB\-\-return\-path\-cc\fR \fI<RETURN_PATH_CC>\fR
Return Path control code (RFC 9503 §4): 0=no reply, 1=same link reply
.TP
\fB\-\-return\-address\fR \fI<IP>\fR
Return Path alternate reply address (RFC 9503 §4)
.TP
\fB\-\-return\-sr\-mpls\-labels\fR \fI<LABELS>\fR
Return Path SR\-MPLS label stack (RFC 9503 §4). Comma\-separated 20\-bit labels
.TP
\fB\-\-return\-srv6\-sids\fR \fI<SIDS>\fR
Return Path SRv6 segment list (RFC 9503 §4). Comma\-separated IPv6 SIDs
.TP
\fB\-\-srv6\-return\-forwarding\fR
Reflector: attempt best\-effort SRv6 return\-path forwarding per RFC 9503 §5 and RFC 8754. When a received Return Path TLV carries an SRv6 Segment List and the kernel supports it, the reflector inserts a Segment Routing Header on its IPv6 reply. Disabled by default; when off (or on a non\-Linux/IPv4/unsupported path) the reflector replies normally and sets the Return Path U\-flag. Linux only
.TP
\fB\-\-return\-path\-allow\-alternate\fR
Reflector: honour a Return Path TLV "Return Address" sub\-TLV (RFC 9503 §5) by sending the reply to the requested address instead of the packet source. Disabled by default: an open reflector that honours arbitrary return addresses can be abused as a traffic\-redirection / reflection gadget aimed at third parties. When off, a Return Address sub\-TLV is echoed with the U\-flag set and the reply goes to the packet source. Only enable inside a controlled (and preferably HMAC\-authenticated) measurement domain
.TP
\fB\-\-micro\-session\-id\fR \fI<MICRO_SESSION_ID>\fR
Sender Micro\-session ID for RFC 9534 TLV validation; does not select a physical link. When set, includes a Micro\-session ID TLV in test packets. Accepts decimal (e.g. `255`) or `0x`\-prefixed hex (e.g. `0xff`)
.TP
\fB\-\-reflector\-member\-link\-id\fR \fI<REFLECTOR_MEMBER_LINK_ID>\fR
Configured reflector ID for RFC 9534 TLV validation; no physical\-link association. When set, the reflector fills this ID into reflected Micro\-session ID TLVs. Accepts decimal (e.g. `171`) or `0x`\-prefixed hex (e.g. `0xab`)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-pps\fR \fI<MAX_PPS>\fR [default: 0]
Maximum packets per second per source (0 = unlimited). Implemented as a per\-(source IP, SSID) token bucket; see `\-\-reflector\-rate\-burst` for the bucket capacity. Kept under the historic `\-\-max\-pps` name for backward compatibility
.TP
\fB\-\-reflector\-rate\-burst\fR \fI<REFLECTOR_RATE_BURST>\fR [default: 0]
Per\-client token\-bucket burst capacity in packets. 0 = use `\-\-max\-pps` (one\-second worth of capacity), which matches the classic fixed\-window behaviour. Ignored when `\-\-max\-pps` is 0
.TP
\fB\-\-reflector\-queue\-capacity\fR \fI<REFLECTOR_QUEUE_CAPACITY>\fR [default: 1024]
Maximum pending reflector requests across processing, handoff and bursts. Full queues drop new requests; slots remain reserved until all copies finish
.TP
\fB\-\-reflector\-shutdown\-grace\-ms\fR \fI<REFLECTOR_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS>\fR [default: 0]
Stop accepting packets on shutdown, then finish queued replies for at most this many milliseconds. Zero cancels immediately (the default)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-sessions\fR \fI<MAX_SESSIONS>\fR [default: 65536]
Maximum number of tracked session identities (0 = unlimited). Sessions use both UDP endpoints, SSID, and optional sender micro ID. At the cap, new sessions are rejected in both sequencing modes; existing sessions continue with their counters and sequence state. Periodic idle expiry or manual expiry frees slots. Lowering the cap never evicts active entries. Defaults to 65536
.TP
\fB\-\-ber\fR
Enable the BER TLVs (draft\-gandhi\-ippm\-stamp\-ber\-07): Bit Pattern in Padding (Type 240), Bit Error Count (Type 241), and Max Bit Error Burst Size (Type 242). Sender\-side only; the reflector computes the counts against the incoming Extra Padding
.TP
\fB\-\-extra\-padding\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
Append an Extra Padding TLV with this many value octets (RFC 8972 §4.1). Uses pseudorandom bytes as §4.2 recommends, for MTU or fragmentation tests. With `\-\-ber`, BER measurement supplies the padding and its known pattern
.TP
\fB\-\-ber\-omit\-burst\fR
Omit Max Bit Error Burst Size TLV (Type 242) from `\-\-ber` packets. This experimental codepoint (RFC 8972 §5.1) conflicts with another implementation\*(Aqs incompatible Heartbeat TLV. Other BER TLVs are unchanged. Ignored unless `\-\-ber` is set
.TP
\fB\-\-tlv\-hmac\fR \fI<MODE>\fR [default: auto]
Whether the sender originates an HMAC TLV (RFC 8972 §4.8): `auto` (default, originate when a key is configured), `on` (always; requires a key), `off` (never, even with a key)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: Originate an HMAC TLV whenever a key is configured and TLVs are in use. The long\-standing behaviour, and the default
.IP \(bu 2
on: Always originate; startup fails if no key is configured
.IP \(bu 2
off: Never originate, even with a key configured. The key is then used only for base\-packet authentication and for verifying reflected TLV HMACs
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-ber\-pattern\fR \fI<HEX>\fR
Bit pattern used to fill the Extra Padding TLV when `\-\-ber` is set. Hex string (e.g. "ff00" or "aa55"). Defaults to the draft\*(Aqs recommended pattern (0xFF00). Ignored unless `\-\-ber` is set
.TP
\fB\-\-ber\-padding\-size\fR \fI<BER_PADDING_SIZE>\fR [default: 64]
Padding length in bytes for the Extra Padding TLV that accompanies the BER TLVs. Ignored unless `\-\-ber` is set
.TP
\fB\-\-ber\-interval\fR \fI<BER_INTERVAL>\fR [default: 10]
BER computation interval in multiples of \-\-send\-delay (must be positive)
.TP
\fB\-\-ber\-bit\-threshold\fR \fI<BER_BIT_THRESHOLD>\fR
Alarm threshold for bit errors per million padding bits, in either direction
.TP
\fB\-\-ber\-packet\-threshold\fR \fI<BER_PACKET_THRESHOLD>\fR
Alarm threshold for packets with errors per million measured packets
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-count\fR \fI<REFLECTED_CONTROL_COUNT>\fR [default: 1]
Request asymmetrical reply traffic (draft\-ietf\-ippm\-asymmetrical\-pkts §3). The sender includes a Reflected Test Packet Control TLV (Type 12) asking the reflector to emit N copies of the reply. Setting this to a value greater than 1 activates the TLV
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-length\fR \fI<REFLECTED_CONTROL_LENGTH>\fR [default: 0]
Requested reply packet length for the Reflected Test Packet Control TLV. 0 means "don\*(Aqt pad" (the reflector will set C flag anyway if it cannot honour). Ignored unless `\-\-reflected\-control\-count` > 1
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-interval\-ns\fR \fI<REFLECTED_CONTROL_INTERVAL_NS>\fR [default: 1000000]
Inter\-packet gap in nanoseconds for the Reflected Test Packet Control TLV. Ignored unless `\-\-reflected\-control\-count` > 1
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-no\-ext\-hdr\fR
Append the IPv6 Extension Header Control sub\-TLV (draft\-ietf\-ippm\-stamp\-ext\-hdr\-13 §5.3) to the Reflected Test Packet Control TLV. Under \-11 this sub\-TLV asks the reflector to add matching IPv6 extension headers to its own reply packets; a reflector that cannot do so returns the sub\-TLV with the C flag set in its Sub\-TLV Flags. Implies emitting the Reflected Control TLV even when `\-\-reflected\-control\-count` is 1
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-max\-count\fR \fI<REFLECTED_CONTROL_MAX_COUNT>\fR [default: 0]
Maximum replies per Reflected Test Packet Control request (draft\-ietf\-ippm\-asymmetrical\-pkts\-14 §3). Requests above the cap receive one C\-flagged reply.

Default 0 disables asymmetric reflection (§5): one normal reply with C set. Set a positive cap (e.g. 16) to enable it; pair with `\-\-max\-pps` to limit amplification.
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-max\-size\fR \fI<REFLECTED_CONTROL_MAX_SIZE>\fR [default: 1500]
Reflector\-side amplification cap for draft\-ietf\-ippm\-asymmetrical\-pkts\-14 §3: maximum reply packet size (in bytes) the reflector will pad up to when honouring a Reflected Test Packet Control TLV `length` request. When the requested length exceeds the effective cap, a single reflected packet padded to it is sent with the C flag set on the echoed TLV.

Linux checks the actual reply route before each send, including wildcard binds and alternate destinations. IP/UDP and SRH overhead reduce the payload budget (1472 bytes on a plain 1500\-byte IPv4 link, 1452 on IPv6). Route/interface notifications and a short cache expiry track changes. Replies are dropped if the MTU cannot be determined or mandatory fields cannot fit. Non\-Linux route MTU lookup is unavailable. Runtime cap updates change this administrative limit; they cannot bypass send\-time MTU checks.
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-control\-min\-interval\-ns\fR \fI<REFLECTED_CONTROL_MIN_INTERVAL_NS>\fR [default: 1000]
Reflector\-side amplification cap (the per\-request *rate* limit of draft\-ietf\-ippm\-asymmetrical\-pkts\-14 §3): minimum inter\-packet interval in nanoseconds. A multi\-packet request with a shorter interval gets a single reflected packet with the C flag set on the echoed TLV. Default 1000 (1 µs)
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-fixed\-hdr\fR [\fI<[SELECTORHEX]>\fR]
Request the received IP fixed header in Type 247 (draft ext\-hdr\-13 §§3.3, 5.2). The sender originates one IP header and permits one request. The optional hex selector is at most four bytes, zero\-padded to the four\-octet Requested field. Nix reflectors return C when raw headers are unavailable. Header requests require a known Linux egress route MTU
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-ipv6\-ext\-hdr\fR [\fI<[LEN[:SELECTORHEX]]>\fR]
Select attached IPv6 headers for Type 246 reflection (draft ext\-hdr\-13 §§3.2, 5.1). Repeatable `LEN[:SELECTORHEX]` values replace the automatic \-\-attach\-ext\-hdr requests. LEN is a multiple of eight (8..2048); the selector is at most eight bytes, zero\-padded to the eight\-octet Requested field. Requests must match attached headers in wire order. Ambiguous subsets require a selector. Nix reflectors return C when raw headers are unavailable
.TP
\fB\-\-attach\-ext\-hdr\fR \fI<KIND[:HEX]>\fR
Attach an IPv6 Hop\-by\-Hop (hbh) or Destination Options (dest) header and request its reflection (draft ext\-hdr\-13 §3.2). Linux/IPv6 only. At most one of each kind, in hbh then dest order. Optional HEX supplies the entire header whose size must match Hdr Ext Len; byte 0 (Next Header) is kernel\-assigned. Default: an eight\-byte PadN header. Attachment failure aborts startup. Explicit \-\-reflected\-ipv6\-ext\-hdr requests replace automatic requests
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-ipv6\-ext\-hdr\-selector\fR \fI<HEX>\fR
Type 246 eight\-octet Requested selector (draft ext\-hdr\-13 §5.1), e.g. 1100010400000000. The header\*(Aqs Next Header byte comes first; up to eight hex\-decoded bytes are zero\-padded. Requires one \-\-reflected\-ipv6\-ext\-hdr and a matching attached header. At least one byte must be nonzero
.TP
\fB\-\-reflected\-fixed\-hdr\-selector\fR \fI<HEX>\fR
Type 247 four\-octet Requested selector (draft ext\-hdr\-13 §5.2). Up to four hex\-decoded bytes, zero\-padded; at least one must be nonzero. Requires one \-\-reflected\-fixed\-hdr without an inline selector
.TP
